WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.427
A join can use a technically valid key and still be wrong for the analytical grain.

00:00:05.427 --> 00:00:09.397
Suppose the target result should contain one row per admission.

00:00:09.397 --> 00:00:13.052
We have already joined patients to admissions correctly.

00:00:13.052 --> 00:00:17.142
Now imagine adding lab results using patient ID only.

00:00:17.142 --> 00:00:24.822
If one patient has several admissions and several lab tests, each admission can pair with every lab result

00:00:24.822 --> 00:00:28.332
for that patient. The admission grain is no longer protected.

00:00:28.332 --> 00:00:34.787
A lab test that actually belongs to one encounter may appear beside another admission for the same patient.

00:00:34.787 --> 00:00:38.137
The deeper question is what the lab record belongs to.

00:00:38.137 --> 00:00:44.142
Does it belong to the patient generally, to a particular admission, or to a specific encounter?

00:00:44.142 --> 00:00:46.592
The database design should answer that.

00:00:46.592 --> 00:00:52.562
If the true relationship uses an encounter ID, joining only on patient ID is too broad.

00:00:52.562 --> 00:00:55.132
This mistake happens in many domains.

00:00:55.132 --> 00:00:59.792
A customer ID may exist in orders, payments, and support tickets,

00:00:59.792 --> 00:01:03.627
but that does not mean every payment belongs to every order.

00:01:03.627 --> 00:01:08.618
Always ask whether the key you are using matches the level of the relationship you need.
